# The company gateway, and the jump host for all three shared build boxes below.
#
# BatchMode, StrictHostKeyChecking and ConnectTimeout are here because it is a
# *jump* host: options on an ssh command line are not passed to the ProxyJump
# child, so `wk status`'s walk of devbox-arm64-2 runs its first hop with
# whatever this stanza says and with the defaults for the rest. The defaults
# are `ask`, no timeout and password authentication, which is a fleet listing
# that stops dead on a question about the gateway -- the same failure the
# bridge stanzas at the bottom of this file carry the long version of.
#
# BatchMode: this gateway offers `publickey,password`, so a hop whose key it
# does not hold falls through to asking, three times, on the terminal --
# measured as `Permission denied, please try again.` under an `ssh -o
# BatchMode=yes` that the child never saw. `wk status` is read-only and asks no
# questions, so the hop must refuse instead: `Permission denied (publickey)`,
# at once. A person who does need to type a password here says so --
# `ssh -o BatchMode=no igalia.com` -- and the fix for the probe is a key the
# gateway accepts, not an answerable prompt inside a report.
#
# accept-new, for the reason `wk bridge ls` gives: it accepts a key never seen
# before and still refuses one that has *moved*, and the refusal is the half
# worth having. A first contact with this host through a probe was going to be
# either silently accepted or an unanswerable question, and "ask" only looks
# like the stronger of the two.
Host igalia.com
  ForwardAgent yes
  User jmichaud
  Port 6789
  BatchMode yes
  StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
  ConnectTimeout 10

Host moose
  User jmichaud

Host buildbox4
  HostName buildbox4
  User jmichaud
  ProxyJump igalia.com

Host devbox-arm64-2
  HostName bbox-12-arm64
  User jmichaud
  ProxyJump igalia.com

Host devbox-armhf-2
  HostName bbox-12-armhf
  User jmichaud
  ProxyJump igalia.com

Host rpi5
  User jmichaud

# tolken -- the Apple Silicon workstation, and the fleet's `mbp`. The two names
# are deliberately different: `mbp` is boot/machines.sh's name for the machine,
# and this stanza is the destination its NODE_SSH names. One address whether the
# lane is driven from that Mac or from another device, which is what the macOS
# benchmark lane needs -- the build, the staging and the run all happen on
# tolken, driven from wherever the terminal is.
#
# The tailnet name and no HostName, for the same reason the bridges have none:
# the Mac is on the house LAN with no reservation, and it is the one machine in
# the fleet that reboots into a *different macOS install* on purpose. Each
# install is its own tailnet node, so MagicDNS distinguishes them and follows
# each across a lease.
#
# The host key is pinned normally. Unlike the rpi4, both installs on this
# machine are long-lived and personalised, so each has one stable key. An
# unexpected key here is worth looking at.
#
# ServerAliveInterval: a bench run is driven over this connection and is
# legitimately silent for minutes at a time, and the session has to outlive
# that. It must NOT outlive the reboot into bench mode, which is why the
# orchestrator reconnects rather than holding one session across it.
# User: `justinmichaud`, and it is the odd one out in this file -- every other
# machine in the fleet is `jmichaud`. That is the account this Mac's *current*
# host install happens to have been created with, not a decision, and it is
# scheduled to be corrected the next time this install is replaced:
# docs/HANDOFF-reprovision.md carries the change so it is not rediscovered
# from a failed login. Do not "tidy" it to jmichaud before then -- the login
# would break, and the failure (`Permission denied (publickey)`) reads exactly
# like an unauthorised key, which is the wrong thing to go looking at.
Host tolken
  User justinmichaud
  ServerAliveInterval 30
  ServerAliveCountMax 6

# The same Mac, in the other mode: the benchmark install.
#
# Two macOS installs on one machine, and each is its own tailnet node --
# `tolken` is the host install, `tolken-bench` the benchmark one, which builds
# tailscaled from source and joins on first boot (bench/mac-tailnet.sh). So no
# HostName, for the reason the rest of this file gives: a node this repo owns is
# reached by its tailnet name and the name is the whole address. MagicDNS follows
# the bench install across a lease and across a reinstall, because
# bench/mac-tailnet.sh keeps that node's identity aside and puts it back, the way
# a board's rescue keeps its bench node's.
#
# There is no packaged macOS Tailscale here on purpose: every one of them tunnels
# through NetworkExtension, whose "would like to add VPN configurations" panel
# only a person can answer, and no unattended install may need one. The daemon
# built from source opens a utun directly, which is root's to do and raises
# nothing.
#
# HostKeyAlias, because the two installs must not be able to masquerade as each
# other: each gets its own known_hosts identity, so a command aimed at the wrong
# one fails on the key rather than doing the wrong thing quietly. WK_MAC_BENCH_SSH
# overrides the destination for a Mac whose benchmark install is not on the
# tailnet yet.
#
# ServerAliveInterval: a bench run is driven over this connection and is
# legitimately silent for minutes at a time.
#
# User `bench`, deliberately not a person's name -- not the host install's
# `justinmichaud`, not the fleet's `jmichaud`. A benchmark install is cattle
# (CLAUDE.md): reproducible from nothing, by anyone, on any machine. Whose laptop
# it is attached to is not a property of the measurement, so an operator's
# account name inside the artifact is hidden state -- it makes the rebuild recipe
# depend on who runs it, which only the next person discovers. Same reasoning as
# the Pi bench images logging in as `root`; macOS just has no usable root login
# to be the impersonal one. `bench` rather than `user`, which in this file already
# means pmbootstrap's account on the bridges.
Host tolken-bench
  HostKeyAlias tolken-bench
  User bench
  ServerAliveInterval 30
  ServerAliveCountMax 6

# There are deliberately no entries for the fleet's boards or Macs.
#
# A node this repo owns is on the tailnet and its name is the whole address
# (CLAUDE.md, "Cattle, not pets"). Every image it builds carries tailscale and
# joins on first boot -- bench systems included -- so `rpi3`, `rpi4` and the rest
# resolve through MagicDNS with nothing written here. An entry for one of them
# could only be a second copy of a fact, and the copy that goes stale.
#
# What that leaves this file for is the machines that *cannot* be on the tailnet:
# Igalia's shared build boxes, reached through the company gateway, and moose's
# BMC, reached through the phone in front of it. Both are jumps, and a jump host
# carries its own options because ssh passes a ProxyJump child none of the ones
# on the command line.
#
# The login user and the host-key handling the boards need are in `m_ssh`
# (boot/machines.sh) rather than here: a board boots a different image on demand
# and each one brings its own key, and that is a property of the machine the tool
# can act on, not a route anybody has to store.

# The tailnet bridges: phones routing a segment that cannot join the tailnet
# itself (moose's BMC port, the rpi3/rpi4 cable). `wk bridge` drives them over
# these entries -- see `wk help bridge`.
#
# The name is the tailnet name and there is no HostName: the whole point of a
# bridge is to be reachable when the LAN is not the way in, so resolving it
# through MagicDNS rather than an address is the property worth keeping. It also
# means these entries work unchanged from anywhere on the tailnet.
#
# ServerAliveInterval because both phones roam between two APs on one SSID, and
# a roam stalls TCP long enough to kill an idle session while the phone itself
# never wavered. That symptom is a keepalive's problem, not a fault to chase.
#
# The host key is deliberately *not* pinned away like the rpi4's: root on a
# bridge is equivalent to whatever is behind it, so an unexpected key is
# something to look at rather than something to shrug past. A reflash does
# change it -- remove the old line from known_hosts when that is what happened.
#
# User: pmbootstrap's default account, and what a pmOS release image's first-boot
# wizard suggests. Password auth is off on the phone (bridge/provision.sh), so
# this is a key login or nothing.
# The `.local` spellings are here for one moment: the first run against a
# freshly flashed phone, which has no tailnet name yet because `wk bridge setup`
# is what gives it one. The image carries avahi for exactly that, and cmd/bridge
# falls back to this name on its own.
# moose's BMC, reached through the Librem 5 that fronts its management port.
#
# Repo-owned rather than machine-local, because the address is not a local
# preference: bridge/hosts/tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc.conf pins it by MAC
# (BR_LEASES) and makes it the entire DHCP pool, so 10.99.0.2 is the only
# address the BMC can have. A hand-written copy of this stanza is how it went
# stale -- it pointed at 192.168.1.41, which answers nothing, and
# config.d/local is read *before* this file, so the stale copy won every time
# it was corrected here. host/dotfiles.sh now drops hand-written stanzas for
# names this file defines, for that reason.
#
# ProxyJump rather than a route: 10.99.0.0/24 is advertised by the bridge and
# reaching it needs --accept-routes, which is off. Jumping through the phone
# needs nothing turned on anywhere.
Host moosebmc
  HostName 10.99.0.2
  User root
  Port 2200
  ProxyJump librem5-oob

# The tailnet bridges themselves, and -- because these two names are also the
# jump hosts above -- the only place a jump hop's options can be written.
#
# What is jumped through a phone, and what is not. A node this repo owns is
# reached by its tailnet name with nothing written down (CLAUDE.md, "Cattle, not
# pets"), so the boards are not here: they carry tailscale and answer to their
# names. What stays is the BMC's hop -- a BMC cannot hold a tailnet identity --
# and `wk bridge`'s own connections to the phones, which pass their options
# themselves.
#
# Options given on an ssh command line do not reach a ProxyJump child. `ssh -o
# BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=4 moosebmc` builds the hop as `ssh -l user
# -p 22 -W '[10.99.0.2]:2200' librem5-oob` and passes nothing else,
# so the hop runs with whatever this file says for the bridge, and with the
# defaults for anything it does not -- StrictHostKeyChecking=ask among them. A
# phone whose key is in known_hosts under its `.local` name and three LAN
# addresses, but not under its tailnet name, is a first contact -- so a fleet
# listing whose output goes to a file printed "Are you sure you want to continue
# connecting" onto the terminal and waited there forever. Non-interactively the
# same hop fails with `Host key verification failed`, which reads as dead
# hardware behind it.
#
# So: accept-new here, for the reason `wk bridge ls` gives for using it on the
# phones directly -- it accepts a key never seen before and still refuses one
# that has *moved*, and root on a bridge is equivalent to everything behind it.
# ConnectTimeout for the same reason every wk probe carries one: a hop with no
# bound of its own is a fleet listing with no bound at all.
#
# HostKeyAlias, and one stanza per phone, is the other half. A phone answers to
# a tailnet name, a .local name and whatever address it holds, and without an
# alias each of those is a separate first contact -- so a key that has moved is
# silently accepted the first time it is asked for under a name that has not
# been used before, which is precisely what accept-new is supposed to prevent.
# One identity per phone, spanning its names, is what makes "the key changed" a
# question somebody gets asked. Same mechanism, same reasoning as `tolken` and
# `tolken-bench` above -- there, two installs sharing one address; here, one
# install answering to several names. After a reflash the remedy is the one
# `wk bridge ls` prints: `ssh-keygen -R <name>`, where the name is the alias.
Host tailnet-bridge-generic tailnet-bridge-generic.local
  HostKeyAlias tailnet-bridge-generic

Host tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc.local
  HostKeyAlias tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc

Host tailnet-bridge-generic tailnet-bridge-generic.local tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc.local
  User user
  ServerAliveInterval 15
  ServerAliveCountMax 4
  StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
  ConnectTimeout 10

# `librem5-oob` is `moosebmc`'s jump host and is deliberately not given a
# `User` here: it is the Librem 5 still running its hand-built PureOS install,
# not yet reflashed into the `tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc` role that this file
# describes (bridge/hosts/tailnet-bridge-moose-bmc.conf says so), so its
# account is not this repository's to declare. The bounds are, and for the
# reason above they cannot be passed on `moosebmc`'s command line. No
# BatchMode: password authentication is off on a provisioned bridge but this
# phone is not one yet, and a hop that refuses to ask for a password it may
# still need is a hop that fails for the wrong reason.
Host librem5-oob
  StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new
  ConnectTimeout 10
